{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. In 1174 he married Echive of Bures who was the heiress of the Lordship of Tiberias. Raymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states during the 12th century. He was a key figure in the struggle between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli as well as in the broader conflicts between the Crusader states and Muslim forces led by Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Rise to Power and Early Rule**\nAfter his father's assassination in 1152 Raymond III became count at a young age. His mother Hodierna of Rethel acted as regent until he reached adulthood. During his minority the County of Tripoli was governed by his mother and her supporters. Raymond III was knighted in 1169 and began to assert his authority. He worked to strengthen Tripoli's defenses and maintain its independence against both Muslim forces and neighboring Crusader states. His early rule was marked by efforts to secure alliances and consolidate power within the county.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Political Role and Involvement in the Crusader States**\nRaymond III was a prominent figure in the politics of the Crusader states. He was involved in the conflicts between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli. In 1174 he married Echive of Bures which linked him to the powerful Lordship of Tiberias. He supported King Amalric of Jerusalem and later King Baldwin IV. Raymond III played a crucial role in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where Crusader forces under Baldwin IV defeated Saladin. He also participated in the capture of Hama in 1178. His political maneuvering and military contributions were vital in maintaining the balance of power between the Crusader states and Muslim forces.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Conflict with Saladin and Later Years**\nAs Saladin rose to power in the late 12th century Raymond III found himself increasingly at odds with the Muslim leader. In 1183 Raymond III was regent for the leper king Baldwin IV. He opposed Guy of Lusignan’s rise to power and worked to prevent Guy from becoming king. Raymond III’s opposition to Guy led to his temporary exclusion from court. Despite these challenges he continued to play a significant role in the defense of the Crusader states. He died in 1187 just before the disastrous Battle of Hattin where Saladin’s forces defeated the Crusaders. His death marked the end of an era in the history of the County of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.22343038022518158}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.41823989152908325}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -7.319182623177767e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.019580528140068054}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00011789103882620111}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.43984711170196533}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.05194463953375816}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00016532962035853416}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.32112184166908264}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.2613789737224579}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -9.894321920000948e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009747759322635829}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0005359405186027288}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -9.298280929215252e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00012158608296886086}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0006754500791430473}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0001215860